        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Value Chain Analysis (Porter) (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Porter's value chain disaggregates a firm into strategically relevant activities: five primary activities (inbound logistics, operations, outbound logistics, marketing & sales, service) that create and deliver the product, and four support activities (firm infrastructure, human resource management, technology development, procurement) that enable them. Margin is what remains when the collective cost of performing activities is subtracted from the value buyers pay. Competitive advantage lives at the activity level: a firm outperforms rivals either by performing activities at lower cost or by performing them in ways that create superior differentiation. Linkages between activities — and with suppliers' and channels' chains — are often the hardest advantages to imitate.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is the classic arrow: the bottom band shows the five primary activities flowing left to right toward Margin at the arrowhead; the upper bands show the four support activities stretching across all primary activities to signal that they enable the whole chain. In teaching, have students write each activity's cost share and its contribution to buyer value — the asymmetries revealed are where strategy discussions start.",
