        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Porter's Generic Strategies (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Porter argues that superior performance requires a clear choice among three generic strategies. Cost leadership: become the lowest-cost producer in the industry and win through price or margin. Differentiation: be unique on dimensions buyers value widely, earning a price premium that exceeds the cost of being unique. Focus: target a narrow segment and serve it better than broad-scope rivals, via either cost focus or differentiation focus. The framework's sharpest claim is the danger of being 'stuck in the middle' — firms that pursue both advantages half- heartedly typically achieve neither, because the organisational requirements (culture, systems, investments) of cost and differentiation conflict.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a 2×2: horizontal axis = source of advantage (lower cost vs differentiation), vertical axis = competitive scope (broad vs narrow). Broad row yields Cost Leadership and Differentiation; narrow row yields Cost Focus and Differentiation Focus. Draw 'stuck in the middle' as the void at the centre. Ask students to place 8–10 named firms on the grid and defend the placements — misplacements reveal the concept gaps.",
