        "id": "A",
        "text": "Strategic business unit.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "justification": "Suboptimal strategic move: violates core unit economics or misjudges competitive dynamics.",
        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Core Competence (Prahalad & Hamel) (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Prahalad and Hamel (1990) argued that firms should be understood not as portfolios of businesses but as portfolios of competencies — deep, collective learning in coordinating skills and integrating technologies. A core competence must pass three tests: it provides access to a wide variety of markets, it contributes significantly to the customer benefit of end products, and it is difficult for competitors to imitate. Their tree metaphor makes the architecture visible: competencies are the roots, core products (components embodying competencies) the trunk, business units the branches, and end products the leaves. Judging a firm only by its end products — like judging a tree by its leaves — misses where strength actually resides.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is the competence tree: roots labelled with 2–3 core competencies, trunk as core products, branches as business units, leaves/fruit as end products. Teaching move: pick a diversified firm, have students fill the tree bottom-up, then test each named root against the three tests (market access, benefit contribution, imitability). Roots that fail get pruned from the drawing.",
        "boundaryTrap": "**Boundary Trap & Novice Anti-Pattern**: 19\n• In young companies still discovering what they are good at — competencies are built over years, not declared\n• To justify unrelated diversification — if the competence does not genuinely transfer, the tree metaphor misleads\n• When the claimed competence fails any of the three tests — most 'competencies' are merely competences (things done adequately)\n• As a reason never to outsource anything — the discipline applies to the competence, not every activity around it",
