        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Ansoff Matrix (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Igor Ansoff's matrix (1957) organises growth options by two questions: are we selling existing or new products, and to existing or new markets? The combinations yield four strategies of rising risk. Market penetration: sell more of current products to current markets (share gain, usage frequency). Market development: take current products to new geographies, segments or channels. Product development: create new products for the customers you already know. Diversification: new products for new markets — the riskiest cell, justified only by strong synergy or attractive economics. The matrix disciplines growth conversations by making the risk gradient explicit: each step away from what the firm knows compounds uncertainty.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a 2×2: Products (existing/new) across the top, Markets (existing/new) down the side, giving Market Penetration (existing/existing), Product Development (new product/existing market), Market Development (existing product/new market) and Diversification (new/new). Add a risk arrow running diagonally from penetration to diversification — that arrow is the framework's real content.",
