        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Product–Market Fit (Part III — Go-to-Market Strategy)**: Product–market fit (Marc Andreessen's term) is the state of being in a good market with a product that satisfies it — the point where the market pulls the product out of you rather than you pushing it in. Pre-PMF, the company's only job is finding fit: iterating on the value proposition against a defined segment. Post-PMF, the job flips to scaling — pouring resources into growth engines that now have something worth amplifying. Scaling before fit is the leading cause of startup death: it multiplies burn against a proposition customers don't yet want. Fit is measured by proxies: Sean Ellis's threshold (>40% of users 'very disappointed' if the product vanished), retention curves that flatten rather than decay to zero, high organic/word-of-mouth share of growth, and improving cohort economics.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a two-layer pyramid/gate: the lower layer 'Find Product–Market Fit' (iterate: value proposition ↔ target segment, measured by retention, Ellis score, organic growth) with a gate labelled 'evidence of fit' leading to the upper layer 'Scale' (channels, sales, spend). A side panel sketches two cohort-retention curves — one decaying to zero (no fit), one flattening (fit) — the single most useful picture in the topic.",
