        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Porter's Five Forces (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Michael Porter's Five Forces framework (1979) explains why some industries are structurally more profitable than others. Industry profitability is shaped by five competitive forces: the intensity of rivalry among existing competitors, the threat of new entrants, the threat of substitute products or services, the bargaining power of buyers, and the bargaining power of suppliers. The stronger the forces, the more industry profits are competed away or captured by others in the value system. Analysing each force — and the structural drivers behind it, such as entry barriers, switching costs, product differentiation and concentration — lets a firm judge industry attractiveness, anticipate shifts in profitability, and position itself where the forces are weakest or reshape them in its favour.",
