        "frameworkBreakdown": "**RASCI Responsibility Matrix (Part I — Business Strategy)**: RASCI is a responsibility-assignment matrix that removes ambiguity about roles in any initiative. For each task or decision, it assigns: Responsible — who does the work; Accountable — the single owner answerable for the outcome (exactly one per task); Supportive — who provides resources or assists execution; Consulted — whose input is sought before action (two-way communication); and Informed — who is told after decisions or completion (one-way). It extends the classic RACI by separating hands-on Support from advisory Consultation. Charted as a grid of tasks against roles, RASCI is the connective tissue between strategy and execution — strategies fail less from bad analysis than from unclear ownership. It applies equally to strategy rollouts, marketing campaigns and cross-functional go-to-market launches.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a matrix: tasks/deliverables as rows, roles/people as columns, cells carrying R, A, S, C or I. Highlight one row to show the rule set (one A, at least one R) and one column to show workload analysis. When teaching, colour-code the letters and run the column/row diagnostics aloud — the grid becomes an organisational X-ray rather than a form.",
        "boundaryTrap": "**Boundary Trap & Novice Anti-Pattern**: • For creative or exploratory work where roles must stay fluid — premature role-fixing kills initiative\n• In very small teams where everyone genuinely does everything — the grid adds ceremony, not clarity\n• When leadership will not enforce the single-A rule — a RASCI with shared accountability documents confusion instead of removing it\n• As a substitute for competence or trust — clear roles cannot fix the wrong people in them",
