        "text": "Publish a blog post showing models wearing the clothes (Interest).",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "justification": "Suboptimal strategic move: violates core unit economics or misjudges competitive dynamics.",
        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Customer Journey & the AIDA Funnel (Part II — Marketing Strategy)**: AIDA — Attention (or Awareness), Interest, Desire, Action — is the oldest model of how communication moves buyers (St Elmo Lewis, 1898), and the ancestor of every marketing funnel. The funnel shape encodes attrition: many become aware, fewer grow interested, fewer still desire, and a fraction act — so marketing's job is both filling the top and fixing the leaks. Modern extensions add post-purchase stages (retention, loyalty, advocacy), turning the funnel into a loop where advocates refill the top. Customer-journey mapping enriches the funnel with the customer's actual experience: touchpoints, channels, emotions, and pain points at each stage — recognising that real paths are nonlinear, multi-device and socially influenced rather than a tidy slide downward.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a four-band funnel — Attention, Interest, Desire, Action — narrowing downward, with stage-conversion arrows on one side and a loyalty/advocacy loop drawn from the funnel's exit back to the top. Annotate each band with a sample metric and a typical marketing instrument. The loop matters as much as the funnel: it converts a one-shot model into a compounding one.",
        "boundaryTrap": "**Boundary Trap & Novice Anti-Pattern**: • As a model of actual buyer behaviour — real journeys loop and skip stages; the funnel is a management accounting device\n• In high-trust referral businesses where advocacy, not advertising, starts the journey — the loop matters more than the funnel\n• When each stage lacks a defined metric and owner — an unmeasured funnel is a drawing\n• For one-off distress purchases (emergency repairs) where deliberation stages barely exist",
