        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Blue Ocean Strategy (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Developed by W. Chan Kim and Renée Mauborgne (2004), Blue Ocean Strategy describes the creation of new, uncontested market space (a 'Blue Ocean'), making the competition irrelevant. This contrasts with Red Ocean Strategy, where companies compete aggressively within existing industry boundaries for a share of crowded, zero-sum demand. The core engine is Value Innovation: the simultaneous pursuit of differentiation and low cost, breaking the traditional trade-off. Companies achieve this by using the ERRC Grid (Eliminate-Reduce-Raise-Create) to alter the industry's standard Strategy Canvas.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a Strategy Canvas plotting Competitive Factors on the horizontal axis and the level of offering/investment on the vertical axis. The standard Red Ocean curve shows industry rivals clustered together. The Blue Ocean curve diverges sharply: it drops to zero on factors that are Eliminated, dips low on factors Reduced, spikes upward on factors Raised, and introduces brand-new points on factors Created, creating a unique value curve.",
