        "frameworkBreakdown": "**GE–McKinsey Nine-Box Matrix (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Developed at General Electric with McKinsey to overcome the BCG matrix's single-variable axes, the nine-box matrix positions each business unit on two composite dimensions: industry attractiveness (market size, growth, profitability, structure, trends) and competitive strength of the unit (share, brand, cost position, capabilities, customer relationships). Each dimension is scored on weighted criteria and rated high, medium or low, producing nine cells. The cells map to three strategic postures: invest/grow in the top-right region (attractive industry, strong unit), selectively manage for earnings along the diagonal, and harvest or divest in the bottom-left region. Bubble size typically shows unit revenue or the market's size.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a 3×3 grid: industry attractiveness (low→high) on the vertical axis, competitive strength (weak→strong) on the horizontal. Shade the three top-right cells 'Invest/Grow', the three diagonal cells 'Selectivity/Earnings', the three bottom-left 'Harvest/Divest'. In class, add weighted-score tables beside each axis so students see the machinery behind a unit's placement, then perturb one weight to show how placements move.",
