        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Perceptual / Positioning Maps (Part II — Marketing Strategy)**: A perceptual map plots brands in the customer's mind: two (or more) attribute dimensions that drive choice — price vs quality, convenience vs experience, performance vs sustainability — with each brand located by customer perception data (surveys, similarity ratings, multidimensional scaling), not by managerial belief. Ideal points or preference vectors can be overlaid to show where demand concentrates. The map answers three positioning questions at a glance: who are our real perceptual competitors (proximity, regardless of industry definitions), is our intended positioning actually perceived (strategy–perception gaps), and where are the open spaces — under-served combinations of attributes that could support repositioning or new entry?",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a two-axis map (e.g., Price: low↔high on x; Quality/Experience: basic↔premium on y) with labelled brand points scattered across quadrants, a dashed circle marking an open 'white space', and one brand shown with an arrow indicating intended repositioning. Emphasise in teaching that every coordinate must come from customer data — the moment managers place the dots themselves, the tool becomes a mirror of their assumptions.",
