        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Kano Model (Part II — Marketing Strategy)**: Noriaki Kano's model classifies product attributes by how their performance affects customer satisfaction. Must-be (basic) attributes cause dissatisfaction when absent but no delight when present — brakes, hygiene, uptime. One- dimensional (performance) attributes drive satisfaction linearly — battery life, mileage, speed: more is better and customers articulate them readily. Attractive (delighter) attributes create disproportionate delight when present but no complaint when absent — the unexpected touch. Two further classes matter: indifferent attributes (nobody cares) and reverse attributes (some customers actively dislike). Two dynamics give the model bite: delight decays — yesterday's delighter becomes today's must-be (hotel Wi-Fi) — and customers cannot articulate delighters in research, since they don't know they want them.",
