        "frameworkBreakdown": "**SWOT Analysis (Part I — Business Strategy)**: SWOT organises a strategic situation into four cells: Strengths and Weaknesses, which are internal to the organisation, and Opportunities and Threats, which arise from the external environment. Its power lies not in listing items but in the matching step: using strengths to capture opportunities (SO strategies), fixing or hedging weaknesses that block opportunities (WO), deploying strengths to blunt threats (ST), and defending where weaknesses meet threats (WT). Because it is simple and universally understood, SWOT is often the integrating summary at the end of deeper analyses — Five Forces and PESTEL feed the external half; value chain and VRIO feed the internal half.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a 2×2 grid: columns split Helpful vs Harmful factors, rows split Internal vs External origin, yielding Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats. When teaching, extend it into the TOWS matching matrix — SO, WO, ST, WT strategy cells — to show students that the grid is the input, and the strategies generated from pairings are the output.",
