        "frameworkBreakdown": "**STP: Segmentation, Targeting, Positioning (Part II — Marketing Strategy)**: STP is the strategic core of marketing. Segmentation divides a heterogeneous market into groups with similar needs and responses, using demographic, geographic, psychographic, behavioural or needs-based variables — good segments are measurable, substantial, accessible, differentiable and actionable. Targeting evaluates segment attractiveness (size, growth, competition, fit with capabilities) and chooses which to serve — concentrated, selective, differentiated or mass approaches. Positioning designs the offer and image to occupy a distinctive, valued place in the target customer's mind relative to competitors, usually crystallised in a positioning statement: for (target), (brand) is the (frame of reference) that (point of difference) because (reason to believe). Everything downstream — product, price, channels, communication — should be derivable from the STP choices.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a three-step arrow flow: Segmentation (market split into distinct groups) → Targeting (evaluation and selection of segments) → Positioning (designing the value proposition and perceptual place for chosen segments). Under each step list its key questions and criteria. Teach it left to right, but note that in practice firms iterate — a positioning insight can redefine the segmentation.",
