        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Lean Startup: Build–Measure–Learn (Part III — Go-to-Market Strategy)**: Eric Ries's Lean Startup treats a new venture as a series of experiments rather than a plan to execute. The engine is the Build–Measure–Learn loop: convert the riskiest assumption into a hypothesis, build a minimum viable product (MVP) — the smallest artefact that can test it — measure real customer behaviour against innovation accounting metrics (cohorts, activation, retention — not vanity metrics like cumulative signups), and learn: persevere with the strategy, or pivot — a structured change in one element (customer segment, problem, channel, revenue model) while keeping the vision. Speed through the loop is the core competitive advantage under uncertainty: the team that learns fastest per rupee of burn wins. The method operationalises 'validated learning' as the startup's True unit of progress.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is the circular loop: Build (top) → Measure → Learn → back to Build, with Ideas entering at Build, Product as the artefact between Build and Measure, Data between Measure and Learn, and the Persevere/Pivot decision at Learn. Draw the loop with a clock in the centre — cycle time is the strategic variable — and teach the reverse- planning rule by walking the loop backwards from Learn.",
        "boundaryTrap": "**Boundary Trap & Novice Anti-Pattern**: 53\n• Where failure is unacceptable or iteration is priced in lives or licences — medical devices, aviation, nuclear, core banking\n• In deep-tech ventures where years of engineering precede any testable customer artefact\n• For enterprise sales with year-long cycles — 'validated learning' per loop arrives too slowly to steer weekly decisions\n• When MVPs would burn a premium brand's trust — established firms must test under flags that can afford the roughness\n• As a licence to skip strategy — a thousand experiments cannot substitute for a view on where value will pool",
