        "justification": "Exclusive legal contracts and historical data accumulation over time represent path-dependent, legally protected barriers to imitation.  ---",
        "frameworkBreakdown": "**VRIO / Resource-Based View (Part I — Business Strategy)**: The resource-based view locates competitive advantage inside the firm: in resources (assets, brands, patents, data) and capabilities (things the firm does well, like rapid product development). VRIO is Jay Barney's test of whether any of them can sustain advantage. Is the resource Valuable — does it exploit an opportunity or neutralise a threat? Is it Rare among competitors? Is it costly to Imitate — protected by history, causal ambiguity or social complexity? And is the firm Organised to capture the value? The questions are sequential: failing V means disadvantage; V alone means parity; V+R means temporary advantage; V+R+I means sustained advantage — but only if O is in place.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is a stepped decision flow: four gates labelled Valuable? → Rare? → Costly to Imitate? → Organised?, each 'No' exiting to its competitive consequence (disadvantage, parity, temporary advantage, unused potential) and the final 'Yes' landing on Sustained Competitive Advantage. Walk one real resource through all four gates aloud — the discipline of the sequence is the pedagogic payload.",
        "boundaryTrap": "**Boundary Trap & Novice Anti-Pattern**: 17\n• Before the firm has any track record — a startup's resources are hypotheses, not testable advantages\n• For explaining short-term performance swings — VRIO addresses sustained advantage, not quarterly results\n• When applied to generic assets (cash, standard technology, 'talent') that fail the Rare test by definition\n• As a defence of the status quo — resources that pass VRIO today can be tomorrow's core rigidities",
