        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Product Life Cycle (Part II — Marketing Strategy)**: The Product Life Cycle models a product category's sales and profits through four stages. Introduction: slow sales, heavy investment, innovator customers, negative profits — priorities are awareness and trial. Growth: accelerating adoption, rising profits, entering competitors — priorities are share capture, distribution expansion and product improvement. Maturity: sales plateau, competition intensifies, margins compress — priorities shift to differentiation, segmentation, cost control and usage expansion. Decline: falling sales as needs or technologies shift — choices are harvest, niche defence, or divest. Each stage prescribes different marketing-mix emphases, making the PLC a scheduling device for strategy change — the mistake it warns against is managing a mature product with growth-stage tactics, or vice versa.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic plots sales (solid curve) and profit (dashed curve, lagging and lower) against time across four labelled bands — Introduction, Growth, Maturity, Decline. Profit turns positive during growth and peaks before sales do, in maturity's early phase: that lead-lag is the exam-worthy detail. Annotate each band with its strategic priority (trial → share → defend/segment → harvest/exit).",
        "boundaryTrap": "**Boundary Trap & Novice Anti-Pattern**: • For individual brands — the curve describes categories; strong brands grow in mature categories routinely\n• As a forecast — stages are clear only in hindsight, and declaring decline can become self-fulfilling\n• In categories subject to abrupt technological substitution, where the curve amputates rather than tapers\n• To time exits mechanically — revival (vinyl, fountain pens) shows 'decline' can be a repositioning opportunity",
