        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Kano Model (Part II — Marketing Strategy)**: Noriaki Kano's model classifies product attributes by how their performance affects customer satisfaction. Must-be (basic) attributes cause dissatisfaction when absent but no delight when present — brakes, hygiene, uptime. One- dimensional (performance) attributes drive satisfaction linearly — battery life, mileage, speed: more is better and customers articulate them readily. Attractive (delighter) attributes create disproportionate delight when present but no complaint when absent — the unexpected touch. Two further classes matter: indifferent attributes (nobody cares) and reverse attributes (some customers actively dislike). Two dynamics give the model bite: delight decays — yesterday's delighter becomes today's must-be (hotel Wi-Fi) — and customers cannot articulate delighters in research, since they don't know they want them.",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic plots implementation/performance (x-axis: absent→fully implemented) against customer satisfaction (y-axis: dissatisfied→delighted). Three curves: Must-be rising steeply from deep dissatisfaction but plateauing at neutral; Performance as a straight diagonal through the origin; Attractive rising from neutral into delight with an accelerating curve. Add a downward arrow labelled 'delight decays over time' showing attractive attributes migrating toward must-be — the dynamic is the model's most strategic element.",
        "boundaryTrap": "**Boundary Trap & Novice Anti-Pattern**: • Without primary research — guessing which attributes delight produces confident, wrong roadmaps\n• When one aggregate analysis is run across segments with opposite preferences — one group's delighter is another's annoyance\n• For long-cycle products where delight decay outpaces development — yesterday's classification ships as today's must-be\n• As a pricing tool — Kano maps satisfaction, not willingness to pay",
