        "isCorrect": true,
        "justification": "**Analysis**: Novice founders commit two fatal errors: (1) confusing Headline TAM (all food everywhere) with Serviceable Addressable Market (digitally connected metro households), and (2) confusing Gross Merchandise Value / Order Value with actual Net Platform Revenue (the take-rate fee).  **Framework Application**: Framework: TAM / SAM / SOM Framework Utilization: The framework is utilized to dismantle the top-down fallacy via three sequential filters: (1) **TAM ($60B)** reflects total consumer food-away-from-home, 75% of which is unorganized roadside stalls; (2) **SAM ($6B GOV)** filters for organized restaurants in the top 50 smartphone-connected cities, where a 20% commission yields **$1.2B Total Industry SAM Revenue**; (3) **SOM ($36M Revenue)** calculates what a new startup with 500 delivery riders can capture in Year 3 (3% of SAM revenue), proving the founder overstated revenue by **33x**.  **Boundary Trap to Avoid**: The '1% of a huge market' top-down fallacy. Top-tier consulting and investment cases demand bottom-up triangulation from price, order frequency, and delivery capacity. THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 6 --- THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 7 ---  ---",
