        "isCorrect": false,
        "justification": "Suboptimal strategic move: violates core unit economics or misjudges competitive dynamics.",
        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Crossing the Chasm (Moore) (Part III — Go-to-Market Strategy)**: Geoffrey Moore's insight is that the technology adoption curve is not smooth: a chasm separates early adopters — visionaries who buy revolutionary potential and tolerate rough edges — from the early majority: pragmatists who buy proven, complete solutions with references from other pragmatists. Marketing that wins visionaries (vision, novelty, disruption) actively repels pragmatists, so ventures die in the chasm with strong early revenue and no mainstream traction. Moore's crossing strategy is deliberately narrow: pick one beachhead segment ('big fish, small pond'), assemble the whole product — everything the pragmatist needs for a complete solution, including integrations, support and services — dominate that niche to generate pragmatist references, then expand segment by segment (the bowling alley).",
        "frameworkUtilization": "How to read it. How to read it. The schematic is the adoption bell curve — Innovators, Early Adopters, Early Majority, Late Majority, Laggards — with a visible gap (the chasm) cut between Early Adopters and Early Majority, and an arrow vaulting it labelled with the crossing strategy: beachhead niche + whole product + pragmatist references. Annotate each side of the gap with its buying psychology (vision/first vs proof/safe) — the psychological contrast is what makes the picture teach.",
