        "isCorrect": true,
        "justification": "**Analysis**: A substitute does not need to be a direct airline competitor; it performs the identical job (business communication & meetings) via a different economic model. High-frequency digital meetings permanently altered the return-on-investment calculation for routine corporate travel.  **Framework Application**: Framework: Porter's Five Forces (Threat of Substitutes) Utilization: In **Porter's Five Forces**, the *Threat of Substitutes* represents external products from different industries that perform the same customer job. Zoom became a high-velocity substitute for business travel by delivering acceptable collaboration fidelity at a 99% lower cost and zero travel time. Because substitutes place an **economic ceiling** on what airlines can charge for business class, commercial carriers must reconfigure cabin density (expanding Premium Economy) and rebalance fleet capacity.  **Boundary Trap to Avoid**: Defining your competitor too narrowly (e.g. only looking at other airlines while ignoring software platforms that eliminate the need to travel). THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 16 ---  ---",
