        "isCorrect": true,
        "justification": "**Analysis**: Entering head-to-head competition with entrenched volume leaders on their home turf (mileage or racing speed) is a recipe for defeat. Royal Enfield discovered and owned an uncontested lifestyle category, commanding 30%+ operating margins.  **Framework Application**: Framework: Perceptual Positioning Maps & STP Utilization: The consultant plots the Indian two-wheeler landscape on a **Perceptual Map** with two axes: (X) *Commuter Utility vs Leisure Lifestyle* and (Y) *High-Rev Racing Speed vs Vintage Thump & Heritage*. Hero/Bajaj clustered in the Commuter-Speed quadrant. Royal Enfield identified the completely empty **Leisure-Heritage White Space** (350cc - 650cc). Under **STP**, they targeted urban professionals seeking weekend escapism, turning a mechanical liability (heavy iron engine thump) into an aspirational lifestyle badge.  **Boundary Trap to Avoid**: Abandoning distinctive heritage assets to copy generic mass-market competitors. THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 19 ---  ---",
        "frameworkBreakdown": "**Value Chain Analysis (Porter) (Part I — Business Strategy)**: Porter's value chain disaggregates a firm into strategically relevant activities: five primary activities (inbound logistics, operations, outbound logistics, marketing & sales, service) that create and deliver the product, and four support activities (firm infrastructure, human resource management, technology development, procurement) that enable them. Margin is what remains when the collective cost of performing activities is subtracted from the value buyers pay. Competitive advantage lives at the activity level: a firm outperforms rivals either by performing activities at lower cost or by performing them in ways that create superior differentiation. Linkages between activities — and with suppliers' and channels' chains — are often the hardest advantages to imitate.",
