        "text": "Diagnose the surge as an anomalous external shock on the Product Life Cycle rather than permanent maturity; use flexible contract manufacturing and temporary variable capacity (Lean Startup) rather than committing billions to irreversible fixed factory overhead.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "justification": "**Analysis**: Exogenous macroeconomic spikes pull forward future demand rather than permanently increasing category size. Building heavy fixed-cost physical assets for temporary demand spikes leads to catastrophic inventory write-downs when the market returns to equilibrium.  **Framework Application**: Framework: Product Life Cycle (PLC) Management Utilization: The consultant evaluates the demand shock through the **Product Life Cycle (PLC)** stages (Introduction, Growth, Maturity, Decline). Management misdiagnosed a temporary exogenous shock (gym closures) as entering permanent mainstream Maturity. The consultant uses **Lean Startup / Agile Operations**: maintain **variable capacity** via third-party contract manufacturers (Foxconn/Rexon) and test market durability before converting operating cash into irreversible fixed plant, property, and equipment (PP&E;).  **Boundary Trap to Avoid**: Confusing a temporary demand-pull shock with permanent category maturity. THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 21 ---  ---",
