        "text": "Explain the transition from 4Ps (Product/Price/Place/Promotion) to Lauterborn's 4Cs: replacing friction with Convenience (home delivery replaces unlocking supermarket plastic cases), Cost to Satisfy (transparent subscription replaces multi-pack sticker shock), and Communication (viral authentic humor replaces athlete endorsements).",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "justification": "**Analysis**: Monopolies often optimize for retailer shelf power rather than customer convenience. Dollar Shave Club removed the acute customer friction points (having to track down a supermarket clerk to unlock a plastic security case for $30 blades) by providing seamless doorstep convenience and authentic digital communication.  **Framework Application**: Framework: The 4Cs Customer-Centric Marketing Mix (Lauterborn) Utilization: The consultant maps the disruption by converting **4Ps (Seller's view)** into **4Cs (Customer's view)**: (1) *Product -> Customer Solution*: high-quality simple shave without paying for unnecessary vibrating handles; (2) *Price -> Cost to Satisfy*: $1 - $6 predictable monthly subscription vs $35 supermarket sticker shock; (3) *Place -> Convenience*: automatic doorstep delivery eliminates the painful chore of finding supermarket clerks to unlock anti-theft plastic cases; (4) *Promotion -> Communication*: authentic, hilarious viral video storytelling creates an emotional bond that corporate athlete commercials cannot match.  **Boundary Trap to Avoid**: Assuming engineering complexity (adding a 5th and 6th blade) will defeat superior customer convenience and frictionless subscription models. THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 26 --- THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 27 ---  ---",
