        "justification": "**Analysis**: Proprietary advanced robotics are valuable but only if organized effectively (VRIO). When automation is applied to highly variable or delicate tasks (like threading wire harnesses), it creates cost and latency. Human labor offers superior flexibility. Realizing that 'humans are underrated' meant aligning human and machine roles to match the firm's True core competencies in battery chemistry and electric drivetrains.  **Framework Application**: Framework: VRIO / Resource-Based View Utilization: In this case, the consultant uses **VRIO** to diagnose that over-automated assembly line robots failed the 'Organized to capture value' (O) test due to excessive downtime. The firm's **Core Competence** lies in EV drivetrain engineering and software integration, not raw mechanical assembly of foam tape. By stripping out failing robots (simplifying the Value Chain) and employing flexible human labor, Tesla restored line velocity and crossed the production chasm.  **Boundary Trap to Avoid**: Do not automate processes just because they are technically possible. Technology must pass the VRIO value-capture test under realistic factory conditions. THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 28 --- THE STRATEGY BOARDROOM  -  COMPLETE CASE CURRICULUM Confidential  MBA Teaching Series  Abridged Edition  --- PAGE 29 ---  ---",
